(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-09"] = {"chem-12-09-m01":{"idea":"A ligand gives electrons to the metal. It is a Lewis base.","steps":["A ligand has at least one lone pair of electrons.","It donates this pair to the central metal atom or ion.","This forms a coordinate bond.","A ligand can be an anion such as Cl⁻ or a neutral molecule such as NH₃."],"others":"Giving up electrons completely makes an ionic bond, not a coordinate bond. A ligand need not be negative, since H₂O and NH₃ are neutral. Accepting a pair is what the metal does, not the ligand.","trap":"Ligand = donor. Metal = acceptor.","answer":"donates a lone pair of electrons to the central metal atom or ion"},"chem-12-09-m02":{"idea":"Denticity is the number of donor atoms a ligand uses to bind the metal.","steps":["Ethylenediamine has two nitrogen atoms.","Each nitrogen has a lone pair.","So it binds at two points to the same metal.","A ligand with two donor atoms is bidentate, and it forms a five-membered ring."],"others":"Monodentate means one donor atom, and en has two. Hexadentate means six, which is EDTA. Ambidentate means two different donor atoms of which one is used, like NO₂⁻.","trap":"Count the donor atoms that actually bind.","answer":"bidentate, since it has two donor nitrogen atoms"},"chem-12-09-m03":{"idea":"An ambidentate ligand has two different donor atoms but uses only one at a time.","steps":["NO₂⁻ can bind through N, giving nitro (–NO₂).","It can also bind through O, giving nitrito (–ONO).","Only one of these is used in a given complex.","So nitrite is ambidentate. SCN⁻ is another example."],"others":"H₂O binds only through O. NH₃ binds only through N. Cl⁻ has only one kind of donor atom. These are all simple monodentate ligands.","trap":"Ambidentate gives linkage isomers.","answer":"NO₂⁻, which can bind through N or through O"},"chem-12-09-m04":{"idea":"EDTA uses six donor atoms.","steps":["EDTA has two nitrogen atoms.","It also has four carboxylate oxygen atoms.","That is six donor atoms in all.","A ligand with six donor atoms is hexadentate."],"others":"Bidentate means two donor atoms. Tridentate means three. Ambidentate is about two different donor atoms, not about their number.","trap":"Hexa means six.","answer":"hexadentate"},"chem-12-09-m05":{"idea":"Coordination number counts donor atoms bonded to the metal, not the number of ligands.","steps":["Count each donor atom that is attached to the metal.","A bidentate ligand gives two donor atoms.","In [Fe(C₂O₄)₃]³⁻ there are 3 oxalate ligands.","3 × 2 = 6, so the coordination number is 6."],"others":"The charge on the complex is a different quantity. The oxidation state is also different. Counting ligands only is wrong when a ligand is bidentate or polydentate.","trap":"Count attachments, not ligands.","answer":"the number of donor atoms of the ligands attached directly to the metal"},"chem-12-09-m06":{"idea":"The oxidation states in a neutral compound add to zero.","steps":["K₃[Fe(CN)₆] has 3 K⁺, so the complex ion has charge −3.","Each CN⁻ is −1, so six of them give −6.","Fe + (−6) = −3.","So Fe = +3."],"others":"+2 would give a complex charge of −4, which fits K₄[Fe(CN)₆]. +6 and +4 do not match the charge of −3.","trap":"Find the complex charge first from the outside ions.","answer":"+3"},"chem-12-09-m07":{"idea":"The charge on a complex is the metal's oxidation state plus the ligand charges.","steps":["Cr is +3.","Four H₂O ligands are neutral, so they add 0.","Two Cl⁻ add −2.","Charge = +3 + 0 − 2 = +1."],"others":"−1 and 0 would need more negative ligand charge. +3 would ignore the chloride ligands.","trap":"Add the metal and ligand charges.","answer":"+1"},"chem-12-09-m08":{"idea":"Naming follows set rules.","steps":["Ligands come first, then the metal with its oxidation state in Roman numerals.","Six NH₃ ligands: hexa, and ammonia as a ligand is called ammine.","Charge: 3 Cl⁻ outside means the cation is +3, so Co is +3.","Name: hexaamminecobalt(III) chloride."],"others":"A spelling with one m is not the name of NH₃ as a ligand. The metal name goes after the ligands, so putting cobalt first is wrong. Co is +3, not +2. The outside anion gets no number prefix.","trap":"Ammine has two m. The anion has no prefix.","answer":"hexaamminecobalt(III) chloride"},"chem-12-09-m09":{"idea":"In an anionic complex, the metal name ends in -ate.","steps":["The complex ion is the anion, so iron becomes ferrate.","Four K⁺ outside means the complex charge is −4.","Six CN⁻ give −6, so Fe = +2.","Name: potassium hexacyanidoferrate(II)."],"others":"Calling the metal iron instead of ferrate is wrong for an anion. Fe is +2, not +3 or +4. The potassium is not given a number prefix.","trap":"Anionic complex: use the -ate name of the metal.","answer":"potassium hexacyanidoferrate(II)"},"chem-12-09-m10":{"idea":"Work out the charge on the complex, then name it.","steps":["SO₄²⁻ is outside the complex, so the cation has charge +2.","Four NH₃ ligands are neutral, so Cu = +2.","Ligand: four ammine, so tetraammine.","Name: tetraamminecopper(II) sulphate."],"others":"Sulphide is S²⁻, not SO₄²⁻. Copper is +2, not +4. The metal should be named after the ligands, not before them.","trap":"Cation first in the name, then the anion.","answer":"tetraamminecopper(II) sulphate"},"chem-12-09-m11":{"idea":"EAN counts all electrons around the metal, including those from ligands.","steps":["Start with the metal's electrons after losing the oxidation state charge.","Add 2 electrons from each ligand attached.","EAN = Z − oxidation number + 2 × (number of ligands).","Stable complexes often reach the next noble gas number."],"others":"Unpaired electrons are not the EAN. Outer-shell electrons of the free atom are not the EAN. Subtracting the ligands would reduce the count instead of raising it.","trap":"EAN adds the ligand electrons.","answer":"total number of electrons around the metal, counting those donated by the ligands"},"chem-12-09-m12":{"idea":"Isomers with the same ligand attached through different atoms are linkage isomers.","steps":["The ligand NO₂⁻ is ambidentate.","In the first compound it binds through N (nitro).","In the second it binds through O (nitrito).","Different donor atom, same formula: linkage isomerism."],"others":"Geometrical isomers differ in arrangement in space. Optical isomers are mirror images. Ionisation isomers differ in which ion is inside or outside the complex.","trap":"Ambidentate ligand gives linkage isomers.","answer":"linkage isomers"},"chem-12-09-m13":{"idea":"Ionisation isomers swap an ion between the inside and the outside of the complex.","steps":["In the first compound, Br is inside and SO₄²⁻ is outside.","In the second, SO₄²⁻ is inside and Br⁻ is outside.","They give different ions in solution.","The first gives a precipitate with BaCl₂ and the second with AgNO₃."],"others":"Linkage isomers differ by the donor atom of one ligand. Coordination isomers need two complex ions that swap ligands. Geometrical isomers have the same ligands arranged differently.","trap":"Different ions in solution means ionisation isomers.","answer":"ionisation isomers, since they give different ions in solution"},"chem-12-09-m14":{"idea":"A square planar complex of type [MA₂B₂] has cis and trans forms.","steps":["[Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] is square planar with two NH₃ and two Cl.","In the cis form the two Cl are next to each other.","In the trans form they are opposite.","This is geometrical isomerism."],"others":"It does show isomerism, so the claim of none is wrong. Chloride is bound to Pt, so it is not ionisation isomerism. Optical isomerism needs a shape with no mirror plane, which a flat square lacks.","trap":"Cisplatin is the cis form.","answer":"geometrical isomerism, with cis and trans forms"},"chem-12-09-m15":{"idea":"A tetrahedron has all four corners equal to each other.","steps":["Every corner of a tetrahedron is next to the other three.","So you cannot say two ligands are cis or trans.","In [MA₂B₂] only one arrangement exists.","So there is no geometrical isomerism."],"others":"Cis and trans forms are for square planar shape. Fac and mer forms are for octahedral shape. This complex has a mirror plane, so it has no optical isomers.","trap":"Tetrahedral: no cis-trans isomers.","answer":"no geometrical isomerism, since all the positions are equivalent"},"chem-12-09-m16":{"idea":"A complex is optically active if it cannot be put on top of its mirror image.","steps":["[Co(en)₃]³⁺ is octahedral with three bidentate rings.","The rings make a propeller-like shape.","It has no plane of symmetry.","So it exists as two mirror images, d and l."],"others":"It is octahedral, not square planar. Ethylenediamine is not itself optically active. A plane of symmetry would make the molecule the same as its mirror image.","trap":"No plane of symmetry means optical isomers.","answer":"it has no plane of symmetry, so it exists as a pair of mirror images"},"chem-12-09-m17":{"idea":"Strong-field ligands pair the d electrons and leave inner orbitals free.","steps":["Co³⁺ is d⁶.","NH₃ is a strong-field ligand, so the six d electrons pair up in three orbitals.","Two 3d orbitals become empty, which with one 4s and three 4p gives d²sp³.","No unpaired electron remains, so the complex is diamagnetic."],"others":"dsp² is square planar. sp³ is tetrahedral. sp³d² would be an outer-orbital complex, which is paramagnetic. Here six strong-field ligands make an inner-orbital complex.","trap":"Inner-orbital means d²sp³ and low spin.","answer":"d²sp³, and the complex is diamagnetic"},"chem-12-09-m18":{"idea":"The ligand field strength decides pairing.","steps":["Both complexes have Ni²⁺, which is d⁸.","CN⁻ is strong-field. It pairs the electrons and gives dsp² square planar with no unpaired electrons.","Cl⁻ is weak-field. It leaves two unpaired electrons and gives sp³ tetrahedral.","So the difference is the ligand strength."],"others":"Nickel is +2 in both, so the oxidation state does not differ. The roles of CN⁻ and Cl⁻ are swapped in one choice. Charge alone does not explain the shape.","trap":"Strong field: pairing. Weak field: unpaired electrons.","answer":"CN⁻ is a strong-field ligand, which pairs the d electrons of Ni²⁺, but Cl⁻ is a weak-field ligand"},"chem-12-09-m19":{"idea":"In an octahedral field the d orbitals split into two sets.","steps":["Ligands approach along the axes.","The e_g orbitals (d_z² and d_x²−y²) point at the ligands, so they rise in energy.","The t₂g orbitals point between the ligands, so they are lower.","So there is a lower t₂g set of three and a higher e_g set of two."],"others":"Five unsplit orbitals describe the free ion, not the complex. A set of four and a set of one is not the octahedral pattern. Putting e_g lower and t₂g higher swaps the two sets.","trap":"t₂g is low (3 orbitals). e_g is high (2 orbitals).","answer":"a lower set t₂g (three orbitals) and a higher set e_g (two orbitals)"},"chem-12-09-m20":{"idea":"Tetrahedral splitting is small.","steps":["There are only four ligands, not six.","None points straight at a d orbital.","So the splitting Δ_t is only about 4/9 of Δ₀.","That is why tetrahedral complexes are usually high spin."],"others":"Equal to Δ₀ would need the same number and direction of ligands. Larger than Δ₀ is the opposite of the real trend. There is splitting, so zero is wrong.","trap":"Δ_t ≈ 4/9 Δ₀.","answer":"smaller, about 4/9 of Δ₀"},"chem-12-09-m21":{"idea":"The spectrochemical series ranks ligands by field strength.","steps":["Weak-field ligands such as I⁻ and Cl⁻ give a small split.","Water and ammonia are in the middle.","CN⁻ is among the strongest.","So the increasing order is I⁻ < Cl⁻ < H₂O < NH₃ < CN⁻."],"others":"The other orders place CN⁻ too low or I⁻ too high. CN⁻ is the strongest of these five and I⁻ the weakest.","trap":"I⁻ weakest, CN⁻ strongest.","answer":"I⁻ < Cl⁻ < H₂O < NH₃ < CN⁻"},"chem-12-09-m22":{"idea":"Colour comes from d–d transitions.","steps":["The d orbitals split into two sets in a complex.","The gap Δ₀ falls in the visible region.","An electron absorbs a colour of light and jumps to the higher set.","We see the colour that is left over, the complementary colour."],"others":"The ligands do not reflect all the visible light. Heat-emitted light is not the reason. Electrons do not jump into the nucleus.","trap":"d⁰ and d¹⁰ ions give no d–d colour.","answer":"an electron absorbs visible light and jumps between the split d orbitals"},"chem-12-09-m23":{"idea":"The chelate effect makes ring complexes more stable.","steps":["A chelating ligand holds the metal at two or more points.","To leave, it must break all its bonds at once, which is harder.","Replacing many small ligands by one large ligand frees more molecules.","More free molecules means higher entropy, so the complex is more stable."],"others":"Lighter and more mobile is not a reason for stability. A chelate does not always have a higher coordination number. Chelating ligands do not need a lower charge.","trap":"Rings are hard to open. Entropy also helps.","answer":"the ring structure makes it harder for the ligand to come off and the entropy increases"},"chem-12-09-m24":{"idea":"Strong-field ligands cause low-spin pairing.","steps":["Fe³⁺ is d⁵.","CN⁻ is strong-field, so the five electrons fill the t₂g orbitals first.","That gives t₂g⁵ e_g⁰.","Five electrons in three orbitals leave 1 unpaired."],"others":"Zero unpaired would need an even number of electrons. Three unpaired would fit neither high spin nor low spin d⁵. Five unpaired is the high-spin case, which needs a weak-field ligand.","trap":"d⁵ low spin: 1 unpaired. d⁵ high spin: 5 unpaired.","answer":"1"},"chem-12-09-m25":{"idea":"A colourless complex needs a d⁰ or d¹⁰ ion.","steps":["Zn²⁺ has the configuration d¹⁰.","All its d orbitals are full.","No d–d transition is possible.","So its complexes are colourless."],"others":"Ni²⁺ is d⁸ and gives green complexes. Cu²⁺ is d⁹ and gives blue ones. Co²⁺ is d⁷ and gives pink ones.","trap":"Zn²⁺ is the usual colourless example.","answer":"Zn²⁺, as in [Zn(NH₃)₄]²⁺"},"chem-12-09-m26":{"idea":"Werner's two valences are the oxidation state and the coordination number.","steps":["Primary valence is the oxidation state of the metal.","It is ionisable, so the ions outside can be precipitated, for example Cl⁻ with AgNO₃.","Secondary valence is the coordination number.","It is non-ionisable, since the ligands are attached directly to the metal."],"others":"These valences are not counts of anions and cations. The two are in the opposite order in the choice that gives the coordination number as primary. They are not the atomic number and mass number.","trap":"Primary: oxidation state. Secondary: coordination number.","answer":"the oxidation state (ionisable) and the coordination number (non-ionisable)"},"chem-12-09-m27":{"idea":"Know the central metal in biological complexes.","steps":["Chlorophyll has magnesium at its centre.","Haemoglobin has iron.","Vitamin B₁₂ has cobalt.","So the order is magnesium, iron, cobalt."],"others":"The other orders give the metals to the wrong molecules. Iron is in haemoglobin, not chlorophyll. Cobalt is in vitamin B₁₂, not in haemoglobin.","trap":"Mg for green, Fe for red, Co for B₁₂.","answer":"magnesium, iron and cobalt"},"chem-12-09-n01":{"idea":"Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the number of unpaired electrons, n.","steps":["μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM.","The ion has n = 5 unpaired electrons.","μ = √(5 × 7) = √35.","μ = 5.92 BM."],"trap":"Check: √36 = 6, so √35 is a little under 6.","answer":"5.92 BM"},"chem-12-09-n02":{"idea":"EAN counts all electrons around the metal.","steps":["Fe has 26 electrons. In [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻, Fe is +2.","Fe²⁺ has 26 − 2 = 24 electrons.","Six CN⁻ ligands each give 2 electrons: 6 × 2 = 12.","EAN = 24 + 12 = 36."],"trap":"36 is the atomic number of krypton, which shows the complex is very stable.","answer":"36"},"chem-12-09-n03":{"idea":"The charge on the complex is the sum of the metal and ligand charges.","steps":["Five NH₃ are neutral, so they add 0.","One Cl⁻ adds −1.","Co + 0 + (−1) = +2.","So Co = +3."],"trap":"Check: +3 + 0 − 1 = +2, which matches the ion.","answer":"+3"},"chem-12-09-n04":{"idea":"Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the unpaired electrons, n.","steps":["μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM.","In high-spin Fe²⁺ (d⁶), n = 4.","μ = √(4 × 6) = √24.","μ = 4.90 BM."],"trap":"Check: √25 = 5, so √24 is just under 5.","answer":"4.90 BM"},"chem-12-09-n05":{"idea":"The complex ion stays whole. The outside ions separate.","steps":["The formula is [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃.","The complex cation [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is one ion.","The three Cl⁻ outside are three ions.","Total = 1 + 3 = 4 ions."],"trap":"Check: charges balance, +3 and three times −1.","answer":"4"},"chem-12-09-n06":{"idea":"Only free (outside) chloride gives a precipitate with silver nitrate.","steps":["In [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl₂, one Cl is bonded to Co inside the complex.","Two Cl⁻ are outside and free.","Each free Cl⁻ gives one AgCl.","Moles of AgCl = 0.1 × 2 = 0.20 mol."],"trap":"The inner chloride does not react with Ag⁺ under these conditions.","answer":"0.20 mol"},"chem-12-09-n07":{"idea":"Add the energy contributions of all the d electrons.","steps":["High-spin d⁴ is t₂g³ e_g¹.","Each t₂g electron gives −0.4Δ₀: 3 × (−0.4) = −1.2Δ₀.","Each e_g electron gives +0.6Δ₀: 1 × 0.6 = +0.6Δ₀.","CFSE = −1.2 + 0.6 = −0.6Δ₀."],"trap":"A negative value means the ion is stabilised by the field.","answer":"−0.6Δ₀"},"chem-12-09-n08":{"idea":"Count the different ways the pairs a, b and c can sit trans or cis.","steps":["Each pair of identical ligands is either trans or cis.","All three pairs trans: 1 isomer.","Exactly one pair trans, the other two cis: 3 isomers, depending on which pair is trans.","All three pairs cis: 1 isomer.","Total = 1 + 3 + 1 = 5."],"trap":"The all-cis form also has a mirror image, so there are 6 stereoisomers, but only 5 geometrical isomers.","answer":"5"},"chem-12-09-n09":{"idea":"Coordination number counts donor atoms, not ligands.","steps":["Oxalate is bidentate.","Each oxalate binds through 2 oxygen atoms.","There are 3 oxalate ligands.","Coordination number = 3 × 2 = 6."],"trap":"Check: six donor atoms form an octahedron.","answer":"6"},"chem-12-09-n10":{"idea":"Spin-only magnetic moment depends on the unpaired electrons, n.","steps":["μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM.","The complex has n = 3 unpaired electrons.","μ = √(3 × 5) = √15.","μ = 3.87 BM."],"trap":"Check: √16 = 4, so √15 is a little under 4.","answer":"3.87 BM"}};
